 ],
 "literal": "Other … milk as feeding / The mother who gave birth is not one who does not want / 'What is education?' says the strong, young man / He of the bright spear is good, that becoming / Those who perished having perished, the relatives of those who remained / Not obtaining protectors, will die / Her beauty will cause a great desolation of dusty heads",
 "translation": "Because she was fed with other milk, the mother who gave birth to her does not refuse the match. The strong youth who asks, 'What is education?' is a good man with a bright spear, and he is a suitable match. Yet, her beauty will cause a great desolation with dusty heads of mourners, where those who perished are gone, and the relatives of those who survived will die, finding no one to protect them.",
 "modern": "பிற ... பால் என்று புகட்டுவதால், பெற்ற தாயோ மறுப்பவள் அல்லள். 'கல்வி என்றால் என்ன?' என்று கேட்கும் வலிமையான இளைஞன், ஒளிரும் வேலை உடைய நல்லவன்; அவளுக்கு ஏற்றவனாகவே இருக்கிறான். ஆனால், போரில் இறந்தவர்கள் இறக்க, எஞ்சியவர்களின் சுற்றத்தினர் தங்களைக் காப்பாரைப் பெறாமல் அழியும் படியான, தூசி நிறைந்த தலையையுடைய பெரும் பாழை இவளது அழகு உண்டாக்கும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Kañci tiṇai and the Maṭkaṭpāṟ Kañci tuṟai, which describes the tragedy of refusing a girl's hand in marriage, leading to war. An onlooker or messenger laments the impending doom brought by the girl's beauty. The mother is willing to give her daughter in marriage, and the suitor is a strong, capable young warrior with a bright spear. However, because of the refusal by the girl's father or brothers, a devastating battle is inevitable. The poet warns that her beauty will only result in a great desolation, leaving the surviving relatives of the fallen warriors to perish in grief without any protectors.",
